From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 21:27:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 159/184] Return to v24, the state the console owner named the
 high-water mark

They labeled v24 "probably the best state yet" -- D: opening in one
shot, files reading correctly, the access light going out after reads
-- and the three builds after it each made something worse. v25
removed the pause on emulator-only corruption evidence, from an
emulator that does not model the pause hover, and the light regressed
to always-on. v26 let the DRV_INIT gate fall through and the TV began
clicking in the same window. v27 cut the screen pump's patience to 90
frames, which force-grabs the sub mid-visit, and D: stopped opening
entirely.

All three reverted. This is v24's driver and v24's IOFW, byte-for-byte
behavior, plus exactly one addition that cannot touch the driver: the
per-op hz_200 timings in CDTEST.PRG.

The standing rule this codifies: the console is the ground truth, the
owner's assessment of a build outranks a headless run, and changes to
the baseline ship one system at a time from here.
---
 bios/scdapi.h  |  2 +-
 bios/segacd2.S | 54 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----------------------
 2 files changed, 30 insertions(+), 26 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/scdapi.h b/bios/scdapi.h
index 41243c6..559db4b 100644
--- a/bios/scdapi.h
+++ b/bios/scdapi.h
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
 #define SCDAPI_H
 
 #define SCD_COOKIE   0x53674344L        /* 'SgCD' */
-#define SCD_VERSION  27
+#define SCD_VERSION  28
 
 /* Everything the disc diagnostic prints, in one go. Taken in a single
  * call rather than twenty accessors so that the numbers in one report
diff --git a/bios/segacd2.S b/bios/segacd2.S
index d6f0521..348d450 100644
--- a/bios/segacd2.S
+++ b/bios/segacd2.S
@@ -249,7 +249,7 @@ _segacd_bios_lost:                      // mask bits seen switched off
         .globl  _segacd_bios_initwait
         .align  2
 _segacd_bios_initwait:                  // frames DRV_INIT may take
-        .long   450
+        .long   1200
 
 _segacd_bios_drvinit:                   // whether to wake with DRV_INIT
         // On, and that reverses a correction.
@@ -608,21 +608,12 @@ brd_wait_init:
         // the one the BIOS's own ROMREAD singles out as worth another
         // go, so "not finished yet" is the reading with the most behind
         // it. Whether it is right is what this change asks.
-        // On timeout, read anyway -- do not fail the visit.
-        //
-        // The hardware log that decided this: the first Getbpb of the
-        // session cost 20,390 ms, which is this gate's 1200-frame
-        // budget to the frame, spent waiting for state $10/$20/$40
-        // from a drive reporting 1 and 6 -- and the immediate retry
-        // re-ran the same DRV_INIT through the same gate and succeeded
-        // in about two seconds. The drive was ready; the gate refused
-        // the states it spins up through. Every stage below carries
-        // its own 200-frame bound, and ROMREADN itself snaps a willing
-        // drive to play, so proceeding costs at most seconds and
-        // failing here cost twenty. Doom never blocks on DRV_INIT at
-        // all: it retries per frame off CDBSTAT.
+        // v24's gate, restored with the rest of v24. v26 made this
+        // timeout fall through to the read; the click on the TV and
+        // "no faster" arrived in the same window. Reverted wholesale:
+        // v24 is the baseline and changes to it ship one at a time.
         cmp.l   _segacd_bios_initwait,d2
-        bcc     brd_awake
+        bcc     brd_fail_init
         move.w  #0x0081,d0              // CDB_STAT
         move.w  d0,50(a5)
         jsr     0x5F22
@@ -789,17 +780,30 @@ brd_fail_read:
         // interrupt frame thrown away. Either way a5 and the scratch
         // stack are the only things it may assume, which is why the
         // stack it has to go back to lives at 44(a5) and not in a6.
-        // No pause here, and the scar is worth keeping visible: v24
-        // sent CDBPAUSE at the end of every successful visit to put the
-        // access light out, and the very next headless run read a FAT
-        // with 95 phantom free clusters and a directory with no files
-        // in it. After a pause, the first sector CDCSTAT announces can
-        // be the hover's sector rather than the target, and this loop
-        // takes what it is announced. Until the loop verifies the
-        // header it is handed (12(a5), CDCTRN fills it), the drive
-        // stays playing between reads and the light stays on. The
-        // light is cosmetic; the FAT is not.
+        // v24's pause, restored on the console owner's evidence.
+        //
+        // v24 on hardware: D: opened in one shot, files read correctly,
+        // and the access light went out after reads -- their words,
+        // "probably the best state yet". v25 removed the pause because
+        // a HEADLESS gpgx run showed FAT corruption after it -- but
+        // gpgx does not model the drive's pause hover (its own sync
+        // and latency gaps are documented all over this port), and no
+        // corruption was ever seen on hardware with v24. Reverting a
+        // hardware-confirmed state on emulator-only evidence was the
+        // wrong call, made worse by two more stacked changes on top.
+        // The hardware is the ground truth; the emulator's pause
+        // artifact is noted and gets to be wrong.
 brd_park:
+        move.w  #0x0084,d0              // CDBPAUSE
+        move.w  d0,50(a5)
+        jsr     0x5F22
+        move.l  _segacd_bios_frames,d2
+        addq.l  #6,d2                   // ~0.1 s of exchanges
+        move.l  #0x60000,d3             // ...bounded by spin regardless
+1:      subq.l  #1,d3
+        beq     brd_done
+        cmp.l   _segacd_bios_frames,d2
+        bhi     1b
 brd_done:
         ori.w   #0x0700,sr              // shut the door before swapping back
 
